        },
        "end": {
          "line": 21,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "description": "Before we look into endpoints and OpenAPI configuration, I want to show off some shared models between these demo projects.\r\n\r\nMost demo's will feature a fake, small conference talk API. This is the `TalkModel` that gets returned by the API, which is an `id`, `title`, `length in minutes` and `room name`.\r\n\r\nTo create a talk, we need to submit a title, length in minutes and a room name. That's the `CreateTalkModel` in the same folder.\r\n\r\nAs you can see, there are several attributes. These are used for validation and to enrich the OpenAPI document."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/Controllers/Controllers.csproj",
      "line": 12,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 11,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 15,
          "character": 15
        }
      },
      "description": "Let's look at the controllers demo! It uses `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` for creating the OpenAPI spec, and `Scalar` for rendering an UI based on the document. Scalar is newer and has cool features!\r\n\r\nSwagger UI isn't included by default, but you can still add it with `Swashbuckle.AspNetCore.SwaggerUI`. That package only contains the UI, not Swashbuckle's document generation."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/Controllers/Program.cs",
      "line": 9,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 8,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 17,
          "character": 94
        }
      },
      "description": "Adding OpenAPI in .NET 11 is very easy!\r\n\r\n- `AddOpenApi` adds the services.\r\n- `MapOpenApi` maps the document at `/openapi/v1.json`.\r\n- Call `MapOpenApi` with a `.yaml` route to get the same document as YAML.\r\n- `MapScalarApiReference` adds Scalar at `/scalar`.\r\n- `UseSwaggerUI` adds Swagger UI at `/swagger`. We point it to `/openapi/v1.json`, because Swagger UI looks for Swashbuckle's `/swagger/v1/swagger.json` by default."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/Controllers/Controllers/TalksController.cs",
      "line": 41,
      "description": "Some demo's use Minimal API's. Some use controllers. This one features controllers.\r\n\r\nThis is the endpoint which creates a talk. You can see XML comments, which helps decorate the summary, parameter and description for OpenAPI.\r\n\r\nThis endpoint is a POST, and it can do multiple things. `ProducesResponseType` tells the OpenAPI document about each of them:\r\n\r\n- It can return 200 OK with the created talk.\r\n- It can return 400 Bad Request when validation fails.\r\n- It can also return 409 Conflict if the talk title already exists.\r\n\r\n`ProducesDefaultResponseType` is quite a cool attribute. It tells the OpenAPI spec that any undocumented status code returns `Problem Details`, the standard for API error reporting.\r\n\r\nFinally, look at the response descriptions. You can set the `Description` property on `ProducesResponseType`, `Produces` and `ProducesDefaultResponseType`. I built this feature in .NET 10!\r\n\r\nYou don't need to juggle a `response` XML tag with your attribute, and you get intellisense for this.\r\n\r\nI highly recommend using this for all non-obvious responses, and errors!"
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/Controllers/openapi.json",
      "line": 2,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 1,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 11,
          "character": 5
        }
      },
      "description": "This is the OpenAPI file generated in .NET 11. It uses OpenAPI 3.2.0, the default in .NET 11. .NET 10 generates 3.1.1, and .NET 9 generates 3.0. Let's look at some relevant parts!\r\n\r\nHere you see a `servers` property. This is a list of servers this API runs on. For example, local, test and prod. In this case, it adds my localhost.\r\n\r\nScroll down to the `TalkModel` (line 315). You can see that this does not make any mistakes with nullability.\r\n\r\nOne difference I noticed is that the `int` properties aren't a simple `integer` with a `int32` format. They use this hybrid string/integer format with a regex (line 324)...\r\n\r\nThe reason?\r\n\r\nIn ASP.NET Core, reading numbers from JSON is set to `JsonNumberHandling.AllowReadingFromString`, which means that our server would allow both strings and int, so this is done.\r\n\r\nYou can change that to `Strict` if you want to return back to Integer. We'll see that in the next demo."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/Controllers/Program.cs",
      "line": 14,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 14,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 17,
          "character": 94
        }
      },
      "description": "Let's go and run this demo and take a quick look at Scalar!\r\n\r\n**DO:**\r\n\r\n- Run this project.\r\n- Visit http://localhost:5301/openapi/v1.json for the OpenAPI endpoint.\r\n- Visit http://localhost:5301/openapi/v1.yaml for the same document in YAML.\r\n- Visit http://localhost:5301/scalar/#tag/talks/GET/api/talks and show off the UI, API client code and test a request.\r\n- Visit http://localhost:5301/swagger to show that Swagger UI works, too."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/MinimalApi/TalkEndpoints.cs",
      "line": 30,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 25,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 37,
          "character": 6
        }
      },
      "description": "Now let's look at Minimal API endpoints with OpenAPI support!\r\n\r\nI have built endpoints that are very similar to the controllers I showed before. The actual functions are separate, so we can use XML comments to annotate them.\r\n\r\n`GetTalks` uses `TypedResults` combined with the `Ok<List<TalkModel>>` return type to provide method metadata for OpenAPI so it understands what is being returned.\r\n\r\n`GetTalk` returns multiple statuses. To deal with this, we use `Results`, which is kind of a union type, to indicate that this either returns an `OK` with a `TalkModel` or a `NotFound`.\r\n\r\nYou CAN also use other approaches like `ProducesResponseType` and extension methods. This is all about preference, and for this demo, this requires the least code and works well enough!"
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/AheadOfTime/Program.cs",
      "line": 6,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 6,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 35,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "description": "This is roughly the same Minimal API project, but now in AOT mode!\r\n\r\nThis is generated with `dotnet new webapiaot`, and I built OpenAPI support into this template. By setting `PublishAot` to true in the csproj, it will build the app for AOT during publish, and enable dynamic code analysis.\r\n\r\nFirst, you'll see we're using `CreateSlimBuilder`. This adds a limited amount of services to the web application, and strips things that aren't supported for AOT. This also creates a smaller binary!\r\n\r\nBelow, you'll see a warning! I'm trying to get a list of all types in the app, but this MIGHT cause issues in AOT mode because some types might be stripped.\r\n\r\nNext, we need to set up JSON serialization to use source generators instead of reflection. At the bottom, we provide a list of types that the project needs to source generate JSON serializer/deserializers for.\r\n\r\nThere's not a lot to show off regarding OpenAPI and Scalar. It just works."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/BuildTimeOpenApiDocumentGeneration/BuildTimeOpenApiDocumentGeneration.csproj",
      "line": 10,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 7,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 10,
          "character": 77
        }
      },
      "description": "Let's generate OpenAPI documents at build-time! For this, we're using `Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi` and `Microsoft.Extensions.ApiDescription.Server`.\r\n\r\nYou can specify different settings for where you'd like this document to be generated. For example, we can use `OpenApiDocumentsDirectory` to save the OpenAPI spec into the project directory instead of `obj/`.\r\n\r\nWe can also use `OpenApiGenerateDocumentsOptions` to specify the file name. Normally, it's the name of the project. However, here we change it to `openapi-spec`.\r\n\r\nNew in .NET 11: `OpenApiGenerationEnvironment`. Build-time generation runs your app in the `Production` environment by default. This property picks the environment, so `IsDevelopment()` checks, `appsettings.Development.json` and environment-specific transformers end up in the generated document.\r\n\r\nThere's a few more settings available, which you can find in the docs."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/BuildTimeOpenApiDocumentGeneration/Program.cs",
      "line": 20,
      "selection": {
        "start": {
          "line": 19,
          "character": 1
        },
        "end": {
          "line": 25,
          "character": 2
        }
      },
      "description": "Finally, this is optional.\r\n\r\nLike I said in the slides, this doesn't _purely_ generate the spec at build-time. It actually executes the entrypoint of your app with a mock server.\r\n\r\nIn some cases, you might want to prevent specific code paths being executed, like databases/configuration which might not be available or cause changes.\r\n\r\nWe can do this with the highlighted line of code. Any code in this block will only executed when we're NOT generating an OpenAPI spec at build time.\r\n\r\nThe document transformer above writes the environment name into the document's description, so we can see `OpenApiGenerationEnvironment` at work."
    },
    {
      "file": "demo1-using-openapi/BuildTimeOpenApiDocumentGeneration/openapi-spec.json",
      "line": 5,
      "description": "**DO:**\r\n\r\n- Run `dotnet build` in this project to generate the file\r\n  - If no file appears in this directory, run `dotnet clean` first\r\n\r\nAnd here we can see the generated OpenAPI file! The description says it was generated in the `Development` environment. Remove `OpenApiGenerationEnvironment` from the csproj and build again, and it says `Production`."
    }
  ]
}